WEBVTT

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.080
What is up everyone? Welcome back to another episode

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.080
of Paragliding Out this podcast. I'm your host Aninder Singh, and

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.880
in case you haven't already done so, go do me a favour and smash

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.160
that like and subscribe button and drop in a rating wherever

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.440
possible. Because as mentioned earlier,

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.600
these small acts of kindness from your end help us go a long

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.720
way in reaching out to more pilots out there and eventually

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.920
making the community a safer and a more fun place to be.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.000
Now, today's episode is based on my personal experience about

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.280
running this podcast. You know, the other day I was

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.440
reading a book called Start with Why.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.920
It's written by Simon Sinek. And for those of you who haven't

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.040
heard of him, he's a pretty amazing writer and a speaker in

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.080
today's time. And this book is all about how

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.160
great leaders inspire everybody to take action.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.400
And I thought that if I'm doing my own bit in the world of

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.400
paragliding, that I might as well put in my best foot forward

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.920
to make a change, right? So I've been trying to ruffle

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.280
through the pages of this book on and off various days.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.280
And there's one thing that I found that when I read this book

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.760
early in the morning, it's almost that I can lock in and

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.440
focus in all my attention on absorbing whatever I was

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.720
reading. And this is very different from

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.280
picking up this book later on in the day.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.160
For example, you know, when I'm taking a break from editing and

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.440
the admin work around the podcast, like let's say

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.800
somewhere in midday, it's so much more harder to focus.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.200
And this got me thinking that why is it so much easier to

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.560
gather my thoughts and absorb everything so clearly first

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.560
thing in the morning? And more importantly, what if we

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.600
can replicate the state of mind during the art of flight when we

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.240
go out and fly our wings? I mean you don't be chasing

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.360
numbers during any regular cross country flight or be it while we

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.080
are racing against other pilots in APWC or let's say when we

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.920
have crucial decision making like when to go for the lead

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.560
points or which transitional lines to pick.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.360
Like mental clarity is paramount in our sport.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.960
So my curious mind got to work and I started digging the signs

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.880
behind it. And to my surprise, there's

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.000
actually a term for it and it's called as the perfect zone of

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.040
clarity. You know, The thing is, in the

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.160
mornings, our brain is chemically primed to perform at

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.080
its finest. There's no mental overload,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.720
there are minimal distractions. And most importantly, The thing

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.000
is that we all go through a set of rituals.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.400
I mean, consciously or subconsciously depends on the

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.320
person. But from waking up to achieving

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.280
this clarity, there's a set of events that happens in a

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.520
sequence and I'll walk you through what happens and why it

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.800
happens and most importantly, how we can achieve this clarity

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.560
on demand so that our subconscious and conscious minds

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.040
operate in perfect harmony when we need them the most.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.320
You see, the first thing that happens is something known as

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.000
CAR and this is also called cortisol awakening response.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.560
And I must clarify here that cortisol gets a bad repetition

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.560
amongst a lot of people because it is known as simply the

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.520
hormone which causes stress. But there's much more to it and

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.640
The thing is a little bit of cortisol is not that bad.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.600
And that is exactly what happens.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.640
First thing in the morning, there's a tiny bit of a surge in

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.880
cortisol level which acts as a natural wake up booster from our

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.120
body. It's basically like an alert

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.600
system which makes us ready to tackle the day and gives us the

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.200
energy for it. And then secondly, what happens

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.920
is that there's something known as morning light alignment.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.760
And this is when the natural light starts entering our eyes

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.360
and this process when it hits the retina, it triggers the

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.000
release of serotonin and dopamine, you know, the famous

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.480
mood enhancing and focus enhancing hormones that we all

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.840
love. And then finally, there's this

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.320
thing called first moments and hydrations.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.160
These are the initial steps that we take after we wake up, which

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.040
of course you know, there's a number brainer that they help

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.400
make our blood move in the body, which in turn sends more oxygen

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.320
to the brain. And then the first sips of water

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.040
that we take kind of restores the fluids and salts that we

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.720
have lost overnight. So all together these events

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.640
like the cortisol response, the light exposure and the movement

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.920
and hydration are essential in creating the peak clarity that

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.720
we get first thing in the morning and in paragliding.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.800
Of course, mental clarity and physical readiness are both kind

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.240
of critical to maximize the performance out there.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.280
So basically it boils down to mimicking these mental and

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.640
physical States and most importantly the psychological

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.400
triggers on demand as and when required.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.440
And here's an interesting thing. When I was researching this, I

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.560
came across a study which said that top athletes like Formula

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.920
One drivers like Lewis Hamilton and a few other elite marathon

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.320
runners as well, they use this multi dimensional approach to

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.920
hit their peak zone no matter what time of the day they are

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.240
performing. So this is super interesting

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.960
because our goal is to simulate the brain chemistry and the

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.400
conditions of this sharp morning so that be it 10 AM or be it

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.000
3:00 PM, our mind is clear, focused and emotionally ready to

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.200
make it count. So here is how we bring this

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.800
routine on demand before we go out for a flight or chase

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.440
whatever is it that we wish for. Now the key here is to start

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.400
somewhere between 90 to 120 minutes.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.280
So let's say 1 1/2 to 2 hours before we strap into our

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.880
harnesses. So the first thing that we need

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.600
to mimic is the light exposure. And the idea here is to try and

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.680
step into the natural sunlight. If you're already there, then

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.560
try and remove your sunglasses for a few minutes.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.920
Like the whole point is to let the photons of natural light hit

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.360
our retina, which basically is essential in resetting the brain

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.440
and tricking our body into believing that it's going to be

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.560
go time pretty soon. Now one of the most important

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.200
parts is hydrating and smart snacking.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.800
Now I'm using the word smart snacking because it's essential

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.280
to not have an overload of food before going out to perform

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.840
because what we are aiming for is a steady supply of fuel.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.480
Something which has got a mix of carbohydrates and proteins.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.520
Let's say for example peanut butter and banana.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.280
Perfect amount of fats is heavy enough to give you a constant

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.960
energy source but not heavy enough to weigh you down.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.880
Or oats or nuts like this is your own choice.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.640
But the point here is to have a light snack which gives us a

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.320
constant source of energy over the next few hours to come.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.400
The idea here is to not overly rely on caffeine because of

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:47.280
course energy drinks like Red Bull or any other like no hard

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.680
feelings against Red Bull regardless of the amount of

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.320
competitions, amount of crazy things that they do in the

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.240
competitions. But the point here is that if

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.360
you try and go in for a caffeine booster before you go out and

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.080
perform, there is a high time, which is going to come pretty

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.280
quickly because the amount of sugar that these energy drinks

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:08.080
have is pretty high. And then the crash that comes in

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.680
when your body is recovering from all the sugar rush, if it

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.080
happens mid flight, then it can turn counterproductive.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.880
So instead of chasing liquid caffeine, I would rather say

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.960
that there's a sweet spot of somewhere around 300 to 500

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.440
milliliter of water so that we are able to replenish the fluids

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:29.040
and salt up to optimal levels so that when we go out, there's no

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.880
threat for dehydration. And now the point #3 is gentle

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.920
movement. And this one's kind of important

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.360
because the whole idea here is to try and do some mobility

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:39.200
drills, like a little bit of stretches.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.960
You know nothing that makes us sweat, but just enough to get

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:46.840
the blood flow flowing. And boosting the oxygen to the

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.720
brain is the key here. So take a brisk walk, just go

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.640
out and stretch it out a little. The point here being that we

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.040
will be sitting in our harnesses for the next few hours and

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.000
before we sit in those positions, we have to be

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.000
comfortable so that we don't have any pains and niggles or

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.720
cramps coming up later on in the flight.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.680
And this is something that I've thought about multiple times

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.960
because in conventional sport, there's a whole warm up routine

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.760
that we do before we go out and play on the pitch.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.880
But in paragliding, it's just like, yeah, sit in the car, show

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.760
up to the take off, strap in and go for it.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.840
And it can get pretty physical depending upon how the

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.920
conditions are. But anyways, coming back to the

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:29.240
topic, the 4th 1, which is kind of an optional thing to do, I

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.120
would say, because it depends on the person and how much they are

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.960
into this. I like to call this as a focus

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:37.600
ritual. You know, spending anywhere

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.600
between 5 to 10 minutes. This could be a quiet meditation

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.720
pose or some sort of a visualization.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:48.320
Like you could picture your take off the first thermal or the

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.160
transition lines that you're chasing or the challenges that

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.320
you might face in mid flight or even your final touchdown.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.280
Like the whole idea is to mimic the visualization in your brain

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:05.080
to suppress the distractions and activate the prefrontal cortex

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.120
so that we are able to tell our mind that this is what's coming

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:13.280
and and we should be ready for it in the best possible sense.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.960
Now, I understand that visualization is a topic which

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.080
can be dived deep into solely on a whole different level.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.160
And for that, the next week's episode that I'm going to do is

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.600
specifically on how to visualize properly.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.800
So stay tuned for that one. But now moving on, this works

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.400
wonders for me. And again, I would call this as

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.200
an optional take because it depends on the person.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.360
Now what I personally like to do is, and, and there's a little

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.680
bit of science behind this as well, is that taking a short

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.080
nap, like a 15 to 20 minute nap. But bear in mind that they

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.920
should not be closer than two to three hours before the event.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.720
And the idea here is that there's a build up of adenosine.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.160
Basically, adenosine is something similar to melatonin,

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.600
and it helps us go to sleep. So The thing is, as soon as you

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.680
wake up, there is a gradual buildup of this thing.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.640
I don't want to get into the details because then we'll go

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:11.440
off track here, but the point here is to clear the sleep

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.040
pressure that has been built up until that time of the day and

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.200
to reset with clarity. But the key here again is to

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.880
first of all, have enough buffer time so that you're not able to

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.320
overdo it. Because otherwise there's a

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.640
chance that some people might wake up groggy if you sleep

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.560
longer or for if some reason, you just take a lot more longer

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.200
to get back into the activation of your systems right away after

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.640
you wake up. So with that, I think I can sum

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.480
this whole thing up into something known as a synthetic

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.200
morning routine, I would say, where the first thing that

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:48.400
matters is getting some light into your eyes, followed by

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.080
correct amount of hydration and snacking, and then a little bit

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.400
of movement like limbering up to get in the zone.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:58.480
And then the 4th one, for which I said I'm going to dive deeper

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.720
into the next week when I come out with the episode on

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.440
visualization especially is a bit of mental preparation

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.640
ritual. And then the fifth one is again,

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:10.120
an optional 1 is the nap reset, but this has to be done at least

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:13.720
two to three hours before the event, which is a bit different

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.720
considering that the other routines have to be done

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.280
somewhere between an hour and a half to two before the event.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.960
So now before we wrap up for the day, I would like to give you

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.480
some insights from some of the elite, at least that I've known

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.720
over the period of time. Because back home in India, I

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.600
used to play hockey at a fairly decent level.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.080
I mean, you know, I never really got to play for the country

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.560
itself, so never wore the colours.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.720
But we still had quite a few professionals working with us,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.600
telling us what to do and how to do.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.080
But let's say that if you're travelling for a competition and

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.200
we have the matches that are going to be played at that venue

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:53.520
at 5:00 PM in the evening. So for a few months in advance,

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.000
or let's say a few weeks, I mean, probably the professionals

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.360
who are aiming for the Olympic levels are doing this months in

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.480
advance. Like the whole idea is that if a

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.360
sprinter has this event at the Olympics scheduled for 8:00 PM,

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.520
then what they will do is that they will start training for

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:13.120
that race every day at 8:00 PM rather than having their regular

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.840
schedule of training at 4:00 AM. Or like, you know, I mean,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.720
that's a weird number, but let's say 10 AM or 11:00 AM.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.000
So the idea is to match your entire circadian rhythm to the

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.120
competition times. Now, the key here is that of

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.560
course you would say that, hey, in paragliding everything is

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.640
when the thermals start because that is when the day starts.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:34.160
So there's no set point of time when there's a start gate

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.840
opening. But what we can do is, for

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:39.800
example, let's say I'm going to Macedonia in a week and there's

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.680
going to be racing for seven days in a row.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Like even though I'm not a part of the race officially, but I

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.120
still hope to be flying in these goggles and learning and

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:51.920
absorbing a lot of it. So my idea here is that if the

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:55.480
start gate in Macedonia is likely to open somewhere around

00:12:55.480 --> 00:13:00.160
11:00 everyday, then I want to start doing these rituals and

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:06.040
these pre game checks at least 9:00 beforehand.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:08.960
So even when I'm not travelling to Macedonia, even if there's a

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:12.120
week that is remaining for me to get on the plane and be a part

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.440
of the whole ecosystem over there, I will still start

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.240
training my brain to getting into the zone at 9:00 AM.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.000
So that eventually when I get there and it's go time, my brain

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.800
is ready to perform at its finest, rather than taking the

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.560
first few days to just kind of warm up and absorb the

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.480
atmosphere and then adapt to what the reality is.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.040
So the idea is that you visualize the reality that

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:39.440
you're going to face later on, and then your brain adapts to it

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.720
much quicker when it actually happens.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.680
And another thing that I would like to point out here is that

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.040
from my field hockey days, we were categorically told to avoid

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.560
any kind of overstimulation before the match because chasing

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.040
adrenaline is going to be there when the event starts anyways.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.960
So the idea is to have very limited emotional or social

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:03.760
triggers or, you know, the caffeine intake, like anything

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.320
that gets us in a state of arousal because we're going to

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.800
need all of that energy later on when we are performing at the

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:14.280
highest level anyways. So the point was to conserve the

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.760
energy as much as possible and up to a point that although

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.360
field hockey was a very running heavy sport, you know that we

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:23.400
needed a lower limbs to be in their top shape because there

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:25.640
was a lot of stress and load during the event.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.200
But why I'm trying to tell you this is that we were told to

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:33.200
mostly sit or lie down so that the lactic acid buildup in our

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.040
legs is a lot less compared to other teams, which were just

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.800
walking around and going on for sightseeing.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.000
And I think this is also visible in some Olympic opening

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.680
ceremonies where you would see some teams are skipping the

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.360
opening ceremonies because of long standing hours.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:53.640
And the whole idea is that the fatigue in the lower limbs can

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:57.400
build up over multi hour or multi day events.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:00.040
So especially in paragliding, let's say if there's a World Cup

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:04.480
which is spread over like 7 days in a row and you will be pushing

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:09.760
bar at 2-3 hours every day, you would want to conserve as much

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:13.120
energy as possible in lower limbs so that by the time it's

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:17.040
day #4 or day #5 you're not risking getting cramps in your

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.320
calves or just keeping your muscles as limbered up as

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.480
possible. So the point here is to avoid

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:27.640
cumulative fatigue that develops over a period of time.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.920
But anyways, with that, I think we have come to the end of

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.880
today's episode and I really hope you found these strategies

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:35.720
useful. Do let me know in comments

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:38.320
wherever you listen to if you have your own little pre fright

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:41.080
rituals or tweaks that we can share with the other audience

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:44.720
members next time onwards. And next week, as mentioned

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:48.480
earlier, we'll dive into how to visualize properly and make it

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.360
count when it matters the most. And this Thursday, again, I'm

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.120
not going to spill any more beans, but we have a banger of

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.720
an episode coming from Battery Lighting Studios, as usual,

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.240
coming your way. So stay tuned, stay excited, and

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:01.000
I'll hope you like it.
